{
 "luot": "l1",
 "chu_gui": "Samuel Pepys watched the fire from Bankside.\n\nThe Thames runs past Southwark.\n\nIt began in Pudding Lane, at Thomas Farriner's bakery.\n\nWenceslaus Hollar drew it from St Saviour's.\n\nOld St Paul's stood above St Magnus and Fish Street.\n\nPepys went to Whitehall, then to Cannon Street.\n\nThe Lord Mayor was Sir Thomas Bludworth.\n\nIt spread up Gracechurch Street to Lombard Street.\n\nJohn Evelyn walked up Ludgate Hill to Fleet Street.\n\nHouses came down in Tower Street, near the Navy Office.\n\nPepys buried his Parmesan cheese.\n\nPeople camped in Moorfields, St George's Fields and Highgate.\n\nChristopher Wren and Robert Hooke drew new plans.\n\nThe Fire Court sat at Clifford's Inn.\n\nKing Street runs down from the Guildhall.\n\nNicholas Barbon opened the Fire Office.\n\nThe Victoria Embankment pushed out into the river.\n\nGodfrey Allen devised the St Paul's Heights.\n\nPaternoster Row burned in the Blitz.\n\nCharles Holden and William Holford drew the plan.\n\nLondon Wall and the Barbican rose over Cripplegate.\n\nTowers rise along Bishopsgate and Leadenhall Street.\n\nThirty St Mary Axe stands nearby.\n\nThe views run from Parliament Hill, Primrose Hill and Greenwich Park.\n\nThe farthest is King Henry's Mound, in Richmond Park.\n\nCheapside burned, as Doornick's map shows.\n\nWinston Churchill sent word.",
